@startuml alert-delivery
title US-03: Final v1 Alert Delivery - Durable Persist + Distributed WebSocket Fan-Out

skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam participantBorderColor #555555
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #F8F8F8
skinparam noteBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7

queue "Kafka\nalerts" as Kafka
participant "API\n(consuming instance)" as APIConsumer
database "TimescaleDB\nalerts" as TSDB
database "Redis\nalert-events\n(pub/sub)" as RedisPubSub
participant "API\n(all instances)" as APIAll
participant "Angular\nDashboard" as Dashboard
actor Operator

note over Kafka, APIConsumer
  Final v1 behavior is shown here.
  Phase 03 proves authenticated delivery through the consuming API instance.
  Workspace scope arrives in Phase 07.
  Multi-instance Redis fan-out arrives in Phase 08.
end note

Kafka -> APIConsumer : consume deterministic SIGNAL_LOSS alert
APIConsumer -> TSDB : INSERT alert status=NEW\nON CONFLICT (alert_id) DO NOTHING
TSDB --> APIConsumer : inserted or existing durable row

APIConsumer -> RedisPubSub : PUBLISH ALERT_CREATED\n(full current alert state)
RedisPubSub --> APIAll : all API instances receive lifecycle event

note over APIConsumer
  Consume ordering:
  1. durable DB effect
  2. lifecycle pub/sub
  3. Kafka offset commit

  Crash after DB write can replay the Kafka event.
  DB row remains one logical alert;
  pub/sub/WebSocket notification may repeat.
end note

APIAll --> Dashboard : push to authorized/scope-matched WebSocket
Dashboard --> Operator : display SIGNAL_LOSS status=NEW

note over Dashboard
  WebSocket delivery is at-least-once.
  Client deduplicates/converges by alert_id
  and current lifecycle status/version semantics.
end note

@enduml
